# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for JackBus test runs.
#
# Only pre-existing accesses that are racy by design are listed: mostly
# fields in shared memory that several threads/processes read and write with
# plain accesses, synchronized (if at all) through futexes in shared memory
# or the lock-free state switching, which TSan cannot see. Do not add
# entries for code introduced by this fork; fix those races instead.

# (Per-client timing records and the CPU load statistics were listed here; they are fixed:
# relaxed atomic accesses to the timing records, and the statistics reset by the RT thread.)

# Coherent reads of the graph state (JackAtomicState): read, then check that the state index
# did not change, else read again (a seqlock). A writer can be filling the state a slow reader
# is still reading; the reader then retries. TSan cannot see the retry.
race:Jack::JackGraphManager::RecalculateLatencyAux

# Client control flags in shared memory (fActive, fTransportSync/State/
# Timebase, fCallback[]) written by API/notification threads and read by
# the RT thread and the server.
race:Jack::JackClient::CallSyncCallbackAux
race:Jack::JackClient::Activate
race:Jack::JackClient::Deactivate
race:Jack::JackClient::IsActive
race:Jack::JackTransportEngine::CycleEnd
race:Jack::JackTransportEngine::MakeAllStartingLocating
race:Jack::JackTransportEngine::CheckAllRolling

# Callbacks set by the application while notifications can already arrive
# (the API requires the client to be inactive, not the notification thread
# to be idle); pre-existing API-level race.
race:Jack::JackClient::Set*Callback
race:Jack::JackClient::ClientNotify

# fTransportTimebase (client control flag in shared memory) is cleared both
# by jack_release_timebase() and by the process thread after the "new
# position" call. Matched on the API side only, so other races in
# CallTimebaseCallbackAux() are still reported.
race:Jack::JackClient::ReleaseTimebase

# Port fields in shared memory: a latency callback can write the latency of
# a port that is being released; the slot is reinitialized when reused.
race:Jack::JackPort::Release
